ks/promote-a-pruned-release.md", "documentation/governance-artifact-lifecycle-requirements.md", "documentation/issues/733-design.md", "documentation/issues/733-requirements.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "documentation/release-process.md", "plugin/CHANGELOG.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/change_cut.py", "plugin/lib/change_fragments.py", "plugin/lib/change_log_archive.py", "plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/templates/change-fragment.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_atomic_state_writes.py", "tests/test_change_cut.py", "tests/test_change_fragments.py", "tests/test_change_log_archive.py", "tests/test_consumer_digest.py", "tests/test_plugin_version_banner.py", "tests/test_release_readiness.py", "tests/test_suite_at_boundary.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-consumer-digest.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/consumer-digest.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/promote-a-pruned-release.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "A declared release where nothing ships leaves this release's headline file in place without saying so, and the next cut publishes it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. In plan_cut, a declared repo whose release ships no fragment returns before _release_writes (the no-pending early return, and `if not cut: return result` when every fragment is withheld). cut_cmd then prints 'nothing to do' and calls _print_headline_kept, but headline_kept is only ever set on the re-run path. So a `.prawduct/release-headline.md` written for this release under `headline: required` (which Phase 0 asked the operator to write) stays without comment, and the next release's cut puts it under that release's heading. test_a_declared_repo_with_nothing_pending_writes_no_release_file pins the no-write outcome but not what the operator is told. Phase 0's `digest headline:` emission on the next release does show the line, so this is a NOTE. The cheap fix: in the no-work branch, set headline_kept with a reason ('no fragment ships in vX, so no section was written and the headline stays for the next cut'). Alternatively, record the no-section-for-an-empty-release behaviour as deliberate in 733-design.md's re-run/output tables, where it is currently not stated.", "files": ["plugin/lib/change_cut.py", "tests/test_change_cut.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Phase 0 restates the cut's changelog preconditions rather than asking one owner, and already covers only some of them", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: consumer_digest owns each fact (read_headline, release_sections, RELEASE_IN_CHANGELOG_REMEDY, release_file_rel), but the sequence of checks that becomes a refusal has two copies. change_cut._release_writes checks is_file -> read_changelog -> release_sections -> read_headline (unreadable, required-and-absent) -> insertion_index (marker). release_readiness._release_section_check and _headline_warnings run the same sequence again and name the cut's codes in hardcoded prose (`digest-missing`, `digest-unreadable`, `release-in-changelog`, `no-headline`). Members already diverge. Phase 0 does not warn about `marker-missing` or `releases-ignored`, both cut refusals that are cheap to fix ahead of time, which is the reason _digest_advisories gives for existing. And the 'already cut' predicate is `release_file.exists()` in plan_cut but `.is_file()` in _digest_advisories. Consequence: whoever next adds or renames a cut refusal edits change_cut, and Phase 0 silently says nothing about it or names a code that no longer exists. Remedy (construction, not a longer list): add one preflight to consumer_digest, e.g. `problems(project_dir, decl, version) -> list[(code, detail)]`, which also owns the 'already cut' predicate. The cut raises the first problem as CutRefused and Phase 0 prints each as a warning. Search `CutRefused(` in change_cut.py and the backticked codes in release_readiness.py to bound the set.", "files": ["plugin/lib/release_readiness.py", "plugin/lib/change_cut.py", "plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "cut_cmd infers 'completing an earlier cut' from an empty `cut` list rather than from a recorded mode", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The design's CD8 re-run table is the model. In code, though, the mode (a normal cut, completing a cut whose release file exists, or nothing to do) is not on CutPlan. cut_cmd rebuilds it from `cut`, `cleanup`, `headline_file` and `has_work`: the `else` branch of `if cut_plan.cut` and the `if not cut_plan.cut and cut_plan.headline_file is not None` line both assume that an empty `cut` with work means _plan_rerun built the plan. That holds today only because _release_writes, the one other setter of headline_file, runs only when `cut` is non-empty. An explicit `CutPlan.completing: bool`, set by _plan_rerun, would turn those branches into a read rather than an inference. Borderline: the signals are few and the logic stays in one module. No change required.", "files": ["plugin/lib/change_cut.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Generality: version headings are recognised only at `## `, so a history with `### [x.y.z]` patch releases gets the new section inserted beneath them, and the docs don't say so", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Framework Check 7, Generality. heading_version deliberately ignores `###` so that a past release's subsections do not attract the insert. conventional-changelog's standard-version output, however, writes patch releases as `### [1.0.1](\u2026)` under `## [1.0.0]`-level minors. For such a product, insertion_index picks the first `## ` version, which places the new section below the newer patch sections, and `release-in-changelog` never sees a `###` heading. The dry run's 'at line N' is the only signal. project-state-keys.md already names `marker` as the escape for `## Release 1.4.0`-style histories. Either extend that sentence to cover version headings at another level, or accept a version-leading `###` heading in heading_version: by-type subsections are `### Added` etc. and never lead with a version, so they would not attract the insert. Either way, add a scope-out line to 733-requirements.md.", "files": ["plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The two new history files are not exempt from retired-claim-survives", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: `retired_claims._is_history` decides what counts as history by path and filename (archives, `.prawduct/changes/`, names starting `change-log`/`changelog`), and #733 adds two append-only history files that neither test matches. (1) `.prawduct/releases/vX.Y.Z.md` is the cut's identity record. It is never edited, and runbook step 20 publishes the GitHub Release from it. Today the same section is exempt in `plugin/CHANGELOG.md` because of the filename, but its mirror in the release file is not. (2) The declared `consumer_digest: path` is exempt only if its name happens to start with `changelog` (`NEWS.md` or `docs/HISTORY.md` would not be). The consequence: the first `Retires:` phrase that appears in a release's notes produces a permanent `retired-claim-survives` WARNING on a file that must not change. The only ways to clear it are editing the release file, which then disagrees with the changelog and the published Release, or adding `prawduct:allow` lines to generated output. The plan considered the unreleased-claims scan (Chunk 05) but not this reader. Core rule: \"Enumerate a set by query, never memory, and bound it by the property that justifies it, not a location\". The fix is to derive history from the property: have `_is_history` ask `consumer_digest` for `RELEASES_DIR_REL` and the declared path, rather than add another hard-coded name. Then check the other history-by-path predicates found with `git grep -n \"change-log-archive\" plugin/lib plugin/bin` for the same gap.", "files": ["plugin/lib/consumer_digest.py", "plugin/lib/change_cut.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Records Pass: the release runbook assumes the headline file is committed, but no step commits it", "recommendation": "Instance (three lines in one runbook). Why it broke: step 2 writes `.prawduct/release-headline.md`, and neither it nor `documentation/release-process.md` says to commit it, so at the cut it is an untracked file. Three later lines assume it is tracked. Step 5 expects ` D .prawduct/release-headline.md` and then says \"Nothing else\", but git prints nothing for an untracked file it deleted. Step 12 expects a `delete mode` line for it, which will not appear. Step 3's undo note says to restore it \"from `HEAD`\" and then delete `.prawduct/releases/vX.Y.Z.md`. Restoring a path that is not in HEAD errors, and `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>` then restores none of the paths. Deleting the release file afterwards destroys the last copy of the headline. This clears the 'misleads into action' bar: the steps run for the first time at the v3.8.0 cut. The fix is to choose one model. Either step 2 commits the headline file, or steps 5 and 12 drop the tracked-deletion lines and the undo note recovers the headline from the release file (`sed -n 3p`) before deleting it.", "files": [".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "write_all_or_none was widened with no recorded check of its four other callers", "recommendation": "Instance. The Chunk 02 decision changes `core.write_all_or_none` to write and restore with `newline=\"\"`, and records why from the cut's side only. The function has four other callers: `change_log_archive.apply`, `change_migrate` (two sites) and `plan_mint`. On POSIX their forward writes are unchanged, and their rollbacks get strictly better. On Windows they used to write CRLF and now write LF. Because those callers still read their inputs with `read_text`, an `archive-change-log` run on a CRLF month file or `change-log.md` now rewrites the whole file to LF there. Hook-surface rule: \"Preserve line endings in any writer editing a file it didn't create\". Nothing reads this as a contract today, so this is a NOTE. Record the caller sweep in the decision, or have those callers read with `newline=\"\"` as the cut now does.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "After a failed rollback the cut still reports that every file was restored", "recommendation": "Class: every caller that turns an OSError from `write_all_or_none` into an unconditional 'restored' message. Found two: `change_cut.cut_cmd` (`refused: write-failed: \u2026 Every file was restored; re-run`) and `archive-change-log` in `plugin/bin/prawduct-hook` (~line 9254). When a restore fails, `write_all_or_none` prints `ROLLBACK FAILED \u2026 do not re-run` and re-raises the original error, and the caller then says the opposite. The release-file model raises the stakes. If the release file's write landed and could not be unlinked, the re-run that the cut recommends takes the already-cut path. It then refuses every shipping fragment as `release-already-cut` (\"written after the cut\"), and the runbook's remedy for that refusal is to classify the fragments `withheld`. It is rare, since a restore must fail right after a write in the same directory. The fix needs one owner: `write_all_or_none` raises a distinct exception, or returns the paths it could not restore, so the caller's message comes from that state.", "files": ["plugin/lib/change_cut.py", "plugin/lib/core.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "No retention rule is stated for .prawduct/releases/", "recommendation": "Instance. The cut adds one file per release and nothing removes one. Only the current version's file is ever read (`release_file_rel(version)` in `change_cut` and `release_readiness`), so the growth costs nothing at runtime. Keeping every file is implied by the plan's 'must stay possible: regeneration from release files', but it is not stated anywhere a product would look (`project-state-keys.md`, `project-structure.md`). A high-cadence product, like the motivating monorepo, may prune the directory as scratch output. State in the key reference that the files are permanent history, kept for regeneration and as the re-run guard.", "files": ["plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions and open vetoable inferences, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. These are recorded in the plan's 'Decisions made mid-build' and the design's amendments. Chunk 02: `write_all_or_none` was widened rather than given a bytes sibling ('every fact has one home'); two new declaration refusals were added (a heading that does not lead with the version, an entry without {note}) ('authority fails closed'); `ordered_notes` moved into consumer_digest. Chunk 04 (delegate B, accepted at integration): headline match ignores trailing whitespace; a non-UTF-8 headline refuses `no-headline`; `release-already-cut` applies even with no release plan; the legacy line fires for legacy scopes classified `ships`; the golden test was renegotiated to a refusal test. Integration: the default date is the local date, not UTC (pinned red against UTC). Chunk 05: `wrong-section` retired; api-contract updated; the unreleased-claims scan reads only consumer notes; runbook #1051 leftovers fixed. Still marked 'owner can veto' at this merge: Decision 8 and A4, which delete two controls (#702's coverage advisory and the seeded-placeholder check); the by-type default map; the default heading and date; and builder amendments A1\u2013A6. Alignment-pass misses the plan records itself: no chunk owned api-contract.md or CDOC3 until reviews found them; delegate B's worktree was cut from `main`; and #1072 makes this plan's chunk states untrustworthy.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-consumer-digest.md", "documentation/issues/733-design.md", "documentation/issues/733-requirements.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: read list and result", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list (`learnings-files --for-diff`): core.md, authoring.md, hook-surface.md, release.md, tests.md. The branch adds or changes no learnings rule, so there is no new-rule pass. Two rules fired, both cited in the findings they shaped: core \"Enumerate a set by query, never memory, and bound it by the property\u2026\" (the history-exemption WARNING) and hook-surface \"Preserve line endings in any writer editing a file it didn't create\" (the write_all_or_none NOTE). Neither finding is an escalation, because neither pattern is a regression this change reintroduces. I checked and found complied with: hook-surface \"A docstring or comment is a claim\u2026\" (`read_headline` and `read_declaration` really are the only readers; `test_the_declaration_has_one_reader` pins the declaration) and release.md's per-section history rule (the `-dev` section is removed, not left). I found no implicit supersession between corpus rules.", "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: #733", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#733 'release-tooling: consumer notes reach only prawduct's own changelog' (#1069 redirects to it). The scope's fragment carries `closes: [brookstalley/prawduct#733]`. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/prawduct#733 status=shipped closed-by=scope:consumer-digest` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says, which is at the v3.8.0 release, not this merge. Cache age about 215 s, no sync error.", "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog hygiene C-B3: open items affecting the changed files", "recommendation": "Scope: none. `affecting` returns two open items besides #733, and no chunk updated either. brookstalley/prawduct#762, 'gitignore: a wholesale .prawduct/ rule defeats the contract both ways': the new `releases-ignored` refusal, which applies only while the record is tracked, is one more instance of that contract, so it is worth noting on the item. brookstalley/prawduct#928, the host-adapters proposal: not affected in substance. Assess and update status.", "fid": "R-13"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 3, "note": 10}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 1000, "scope": "consumer-digest", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["07"], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": "07", "base_reviewed": "0964afa10cdadf2de03264ab992330914cb50ff3", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-consumer-digest.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/release-plan-v3.8.0.md", ".prawduct/changes/consumer-digest.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/runbooks/cut-and-publish-a-plugin-release.md", ".prawduct/runbooks/promote-a-pruned-release.md", "documentation/governance-artifact-lifecycle-requirements.md", "documentation/issues/733-design.md", "documentation/issues/733-requirements.md", "documentation/project-structure.md", "documentation/release-process.md", "plugi